{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-332714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+9y=6x-5.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-9{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-9\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-9x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-9x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-9x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-9x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9x)"] ["math" "A{e}^{-18x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{-9x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{18x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{9x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "A{e}^{-9x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+8y=-13.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+8{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-8{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-8\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -8\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -8\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-8x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-8x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-8x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-8x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-8x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)"] ["math" "A{e}^{-16x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{-8x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{16x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{8x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "A{e}^{-8x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7y=3x+4.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-7x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-7x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-7x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)"] ["math" "A{e}^{-14x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{-7x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{14x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{7x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "A{e}^{-7x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=3{x}^{2}-2x+5.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-331877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)"] ["math" "A{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "A{e}^{4x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-x+6.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332704" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332704" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)"] ["math" "A{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{-8x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "A{e}^{8x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332704" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "A{e}^{4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=-20.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha =-20\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332295" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332295" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=12.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332265" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332265" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=8x+9.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +8(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x+9\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n8\\alpha x+(\\alpha +8\\beta )=8x+9.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +8\\beta =9\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=15.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =15\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332289" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332289" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3{x}^{2}+2x+1"] ["math" "-{x}^{2}+2x+1"] ["math" "-{x}^{2}+2x-1"] ["math" "-{x}^{2}-2x+1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+2x+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{x}^{2}+2x+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=-{x}^{2}+6x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+6x-7\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -\\beta )x+(\\beta -\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+6x-7\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta -\\gamma =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =-4,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-4x+3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x+3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+2{x}^{2}-4x+3"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-2x+3"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x+3"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x-3"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-4x+3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x+3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=9x-3.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha x+(\\alpha -3\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -3\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332376" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332376" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{3x}-3x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=6.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+3\\beta =6\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332543" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332543" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{-3x}+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4{x}^{2}-4x+1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\beta -2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha -2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta -2\\gamma =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-331920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-4x+1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-x+4"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}-4x+4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-331920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-30.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 0+6\\beta =-30\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x}-5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{-6x}-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4,\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-2\\beta =4\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2 & =5 \\\\ A-2 & =5 \\\\ A & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=7{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-333057" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333057" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7e^{2x}-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2x+7,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2x+7\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -(\\alpha x+\\beta )=2x+7\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha x+(\\alpha -\\beta )=2x+7\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -\\beta =7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =-9.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x-9.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2x-9.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2(0)-9 & =3 \\\\ A-9 & =3 \\\\ A & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=12\\,{e}^{x}-2x-9.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-333052" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333052" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12e^{x}-2x-9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4{x}^{2}-3x+2,\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4{x}^{2}-3x+2\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-3x+2.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(\\beta +2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-3x+2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta =-3\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta +2\\gamma =2\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =-\\frac{7}{2},$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{11}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2(0)}+2(0)^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}(0)+\\frac{11}{4} & =-2 \\\\ A+\\frac{11}{4} & =-2 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{19}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{19}{4}{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-333045" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333045" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-4{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+2"] ["math" "y=-9{e}^{-2x}-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+7"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{19}{4}{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{11}{4}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{19}{4}{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{2}x-\\frac{11}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-333045" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\frac{19}{4}{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\