ution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2 & =0 \\\\ A-2 & =0 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\,{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332780" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332780" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^{6x}-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=2{x}^{2}+3,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=2{x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=2{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(\\beta -2\\gamma )=2{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =2\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =0\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -2\\gamma =3\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,$ $\\beta =-1,$ and $\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}-x-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{2(0)}-(0)^{2}-(0)-2 & =1 \\\\ A-2 & =1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d864df0d7a6bddf2201f7ad05cf3dc36449a11ca45e81d57e1869877087c316d"} {"question" "q-332988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x-1"] ["math" "y=3{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+x-2"] ["math" "y=3{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x-2"] ["math" "y=4{e}^{2x}-3{x}^{2}-x-3"] ["math" "y={e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "q-332988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=3{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/state.json" "file_sha256" "53f8c4cba7b7cd509dce07c95fa1ee8f72c79dbdd34bab51cfa6c8e6be5ef732"} {"question" "e-21611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' + y = -10.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/example-21611.json" "file_sha256" "22d198752544d0202dc5047e4a96fa2e29ffcdef8b36bfe9699eb9ecb7521e4b"} {"question" "e-21611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny'_c + y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_c}{\\textrm{d}x} = -y_c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y_c} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y_c} {\\textrm{d}x} = -1\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make $y_c$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K= e^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A = \\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/example-21611.json" "file_sha256" "22d198752544d0202dc5047e4a96fa2e29ffcdef8b36bfe9699eb9ecb7521e4b"} {"question" "e-21612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} +  y = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/example-21612.json" "file_sha256" "a140ff448d381b06670abb598e48399f5c5e78f3758072856e71aab5a6b97058"} {"question" "e-21612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha x+\\beta.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta) + (\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha x^2 + (2\\alpha+ \\beta)x + (\\beta+ \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=1, \\beta=0, \\gamma=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = x ^ 2 - 3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/example-21612.json" "file_sha256" "a140ff448d381b06670abb598e48399f5c5e78f3758072856e71aab5a6b97058"} {"question" "e-21613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/example-21613.json" "file_sha256" "5e1cae1ec9feb2dd20d1a6e0be9db3b2db5b5e259e142a5f82dbe2b817d5b570"} {"question" "e-21613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x + (\\alpha +2\\beta) = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-1, \\,\\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x + 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{-2x} - x + 3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/example-21613.json" "file_sha256" "5e1cae1ec9feb2dd20d1a6e0be9db3b2db5b5e259e142a5f82dbe2b817d5b570"} {"question" "e-21614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 4y = -8x + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 4y = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{-4x},$ solve the initial value problem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/example-21614.json" "file_sha256" "0d033755df75b393b4fa58336c82d860ac12fcfb8e0203b03b7df7ce477f41b3"} {"question" "e-21614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 4y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + 4(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha x + (\\alpha + 4\\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +4\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-2, \\,\\beta = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)= -2x + 2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-4x} -2x + 2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) = 1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-4(0)}-2(0)+2 & =1 \\\\ A+2 & =1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny \n=  -e^{-4x} - 2x + 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037647/example-21614.json" "file_sha256" "0d033755df75b393b4fa58336c82d860ac12fcfb8e0203b03b7df7ce477f41b3"}] "usage" {"e-21614" [] "e-21613" [] "e-21611" [] "q-332265" [] "q-332543" [] "q-332701" [] "e-21612" []} "basis" 1334 "retractions" [[17592186229597 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{\\prime } + 7y = 3x + 4$.\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186229597 :question/worked-solution "The complementary solution solves the homogeneous equation $y_c^{\\prime}+7y_c=0$. Separating a nonzero solution gives $dy_c/y_c=-7\\,dx$, so $y_c=Ae^{-7x}$. Allowing $A=0$ also includes the zero solution."] [17592186229577 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{\\prime } + 6y = 12$.\n\nFind the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186229577 :question/worked-solution "For a constant forcing term, try a constant particular solution $y_p=B$. Then $y_p^{\\prime}=0$, and substitution gives $6B=12$. Hence $y_p(x)=2$."] [17592186229577 :question/difficulty 17592186045465] [17592186229593 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 3y = 6$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-3x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186229593 :question/worked-solution "Try the constant particular solution $y_p=B$. The equation gives $3B=6$, so $B=2$. Adding the complementary solution yields $y(x)=Ae^{-3x}+2$, with arbitrary constant $A$."] [17592186229593 :question/difficulty 17592186045465] [17592186228844 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y' + y = -10$.\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ to this equation."] [17592186228844 :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny'_c + y_c = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_c}{\\textrm{d}x} = -y_c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y_c} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y_c} {\\textrm{d}x} = -1\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{1}{y_{c}}\\frac{dy_{c}}{dx}dx &= \\int  - 1dx \\\\\n\\int \\frac{1}{y_{c}}dy_{c} &= \\int  - 1dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid y_{c} \\mid &= -x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make $y_c$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid y_{c} \\mid &= e^{-x + C} \\\\\n&= e^{-x}e^{C} \\\\\n&= Ke^{-x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K= e^{C}$.\n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A = \\pm K$. Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary real constant. Taking $A=0$ also includes the zero solution, which was excluded during division by $y_c$."] [17592186228845 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + y = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3$.\nFind the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation."] [17592186228845 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2$, i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha x+\\beta\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta) + (\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha x^2 + (2\\alpha+ \\beta)x + (\\beta+ \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\\alpha=1,\\\\2\\alpha+\\beta=2,\\\\\\beta+\\gamma=-3.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1, \\beta=0, \\gamma=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = x ^ 2 - 3\n$$"] [17592186228846 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = -2x + 5$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186228846 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1$, i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -2x + 5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x + (\\alpha +2\\beta) = -2x + 5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}2\\alpha=-2,\\\\\\alpha+2\\beta=5.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=-1, \\,\\beta=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x + 3\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-2x} - x + 3\n$$"] [17592186228847 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' + 4y = -8x + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 1$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y' + 4y = -8x + 6$\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{-4x}$, solve the initial value problem."] [17592186228847 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 4y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1$. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1$, i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$, and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + 4(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha x + (\\alpha + 4\\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}4\\alpha=-8,\\\\\\alpha+4\\beta=6.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=-2, \\,\\beta = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)= -2x + 2\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-4x} -2x + 2\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) = 1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAe^{-4(0)} - 2(0) + 2 &= 1 \\\\\nA + 2 &= 1 \\\\\nA &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -e^{-4x} - 2x + 2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186228057 17592186228058 17592186228059 17592186228060 17592186228061 17592186238166 17592186238170] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228010 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228010 :answer-field/id] [17592186228010 :answer-field/key] [17592186228010 :answer-field/type] [17592186228047 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228047 :answer-field/id] [17592186228047 :answer-field/key] [17592186228047 :answer-field/type] [17592186228056 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228056 :answer-field/id] [17592186228056 :answer-field/key] [17592186228056 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
